On this, I globally agree with Derek.

In my first games, I also tried to get all the armor down to the shop, ...  
I still carry more potion/scrolls with me that he advised (for price-iding  
because I still find boring to get up to level 1 and down again to the  
shop after finding it).

But at least, I've learned to drop my spellbooks and not carry them with  
me :-)

Anyway, someone said one day that it is far better to start with low STR  
(and another high stat) than the converse. STR is just so easy to exercise  
that you'll get plenty of it whatever you do (more or less) so better  
start with a high stat that is hard to raise...

Carrying capacity can be a pain for some classes. That's why wizards  
should drop their spellbooks at once. That's also a problem with knights  
which is harder to solve (dropping the armor is a huge AC loss... I  
usually drop the lance because switching from sword to lance can take some  
time and with knight's AC (and not getting surrounded), early game critter  
are not a problem (then, I usually switch armor once I find something not  
too bad (eg not necessarily mithril)). Of course, YMMV.

But anyway, I now that my stats will be maxed out somewhere during the mid  
game simply due to a couple of blessed gain abilities that I found and  
store (before blessing). In the early game, I have other things to matter  
(such as securing food, and getting a decent AC before the ants).

With a fighter class I might go to the mines first (not too hard and can  
give a couple of XL and interesting gear, plus the guaranteed altar and  
shops). With a weaker clas, I'll usually do soko first (STR train + no  
open space). In Slash'em I tend to go more on soko (shops in the mall).

Another thing I thought important at first and stop doing a long time ago  
was drinking in foutains. I drunk to test whether the fountain was magic  
or not and get the stat boost. No, I've completely stop doing this and out  
of extreme situations I only use fountains with pick-axe of longswords.

Well, to make it short, I agree with Derek, training stats is pointless in  
the long run. And at the only time where it could make a difference, there  
are more important goals to achieve.

>> In order to take your advice about focusing on Good AC (AC0), I will
>> probably have to wear armor. That's about 200-300 CC. A decent weapon  
>> also
>> requires an additional 50-100 CC.

Yes. That makes a total carrying capacity around 400-500 which almost  
everybody has.
Anything more, stash it if you want but don't carry it with you.
Reading a (truly) unknown scroll in the (last) hope that it is teleport is  
useless. One should not have gotten struck in that situation. Of course,  
it is easier said than done but learning to do it is (imho) part of  
learning how to play.
Same goes with drinking unknown potions in the hope of healing, ...

And of course, wands are never totally unknown and you can carry some of  
them, but they're not that frequent before soko (and I still need to learn  
to use offensive wands early, no need stashing tens of wands of MM or cold  
and never using them...)

